1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,320
This is the revision for module three, Data Quality Engineering and Anomaly Management.

2
00:00:06,320 --> 00:00:07,483
The takeaway is:

3
00:00:07,483 --> 00:00:10,170
create a reliable analytical dataset.

4
00:00:10,170 --> 00:00:16,200
We recap lectures fifteen to twenty two and finish with questions to test yourself.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:20,370
Lectures fifteen and sixteen, data quality.

6
00:00:20,370 --> 00:00:26,093
The four dimensions are accuracy, completeness, consistency and validity.

7
00:00:26,093 --> 00:00:31,031
Validity is checked against rules and accuracy against reality.

8
00:00:31,031 --> 00:00:36,831
Integrate sources on a key, align schemas, resolve conflicts and re-audit.

9
00:00:36,831 --> 00:00:40,202
Detect exact, same-key and near duplicates.

10
00:00:40,202 --> 00:00:47,648
Standardise encoding, but standardising can create new duplicates, so re-audit after every fix.

11
00:00:48,448 --> 00:00:52,177
Lectures seventeen and eighteen, missing values.

12
00:00:52,177 --> 00:00:56,217
The three mechanisms are M C A R, M A R and M N A R.

13
00:00:56,217 --> 00:01:00,490
Prove M A R with a crosstab against an observed column.

14
00:01:00,490 --> 00:01:03,520
Convert disguised missing values first.

15
00:01:03,520 --> 00:01:12,688
Compare mean, median, K N N and regression imputation by the variance each retains, since every fill shrinks variance.

16
00:01:13,488 --> 00:01:16,724
Lectures nineteen and twenty, outliers.

17
00:01:16,724 --> 00:01:24,608
They come from mistakes, rare real values, limits, or a different population, so ask why first.

18
00:01:24,608 --> 00:01:31,579
Z-score works for bell-shaped data but masks outliers in long tails; I Q R is safer.

19
00:01:31,579 --> 00:01:37,636
Models like Isolation Forest, L O F and D B S C A N look at many columns.

20
00:01:37,636 --> 00:01:38,715
Fix by cause:

21
00:01:38,715 --> 00:01:44,856
remove, cap, winsorise, impute, log or flag, and compare before and after.

22
00:01:45,656 --> 00:01:49,938
Lectures twenty one and twenty two, encoding and imbalance.

23
00:01:49,938 --> 00:01:51,390
Models need numbers.

24
00:01:51,390 --> 00:02:05,252
Use one-hot for nominal data and your own map for ordinal data, keep k minus one dummies, use frequency or target encoding for many categories, and use training rows only for target encoding.

25
00:02:05,252 --> 00:02:12,728
For rare classes, stratify, use class weights or resampling on training rows only, and judge by recall.

26
00:02:13,528 --> 00:02:15,293
The labs for this module:

27
00:02:15,293 --> 00:02:21,433
practicals fifteen and sixteen audit, integrate, dedupe and scale a merged Airbnb file.

28
00:02:21,433 --> 00:02:25,739
Practicals seventeen and eighteen compare imputation methods.

29
00:02:25,739 --> 00:02:32,868
Practicals nineteen and twenty detect and treat outliers and measure model accuracy before and after.

30
00:02:32,868 --> 00:02:37,456
Practicals twenty one and twenty two encode and handle imbalance.

31
00:02:38,256 --> 00:02:40,126
Test yourself, part one.

32
00:02:40,126 --> 00:02:41,139
Question one:

33
00:02:41,139 --> 00:02:44,489
every missing review rate has zero reviews.

34
00:02:44,489 --> 00:02:47,762
Which mechanism, and how did you prove it?

35
00:02:47,762 --> 00:02:48,307
Answer:

36
00:02:48,307 --> 00:02:52,982
M A R, proved by a crosstab of missing against zero reviews.

37
00:02:52,982 --> 00:02:53,995
Question two:

38
00:02:53,995 --> 00:02:58,670
why does Z-score miss many outliers in a long-tailed column?

39
00:02:58,670 --> 00:02:59,216
Answer:

40
00:02:59,216 --> 00:03:06,384
the outliers inflate the standard deviation and push the limit out, so they mask themselves.

41
00:03:07,184 --> 00:03:09,143
Test yourself, part two.

42
00:03:09,143 --> 00:03:10,368
Question three:

43
00:03:10,368 --> 00:03:15,429
after title-casing a column, drop duplicates removes new rows.

44
00:03:15,429 --> 00:03:15,755
Why?

45
00:03:15,755 --> 00:03:16,326
Answer:

46
00:03:16,326 --> 00:03:23,428
lowercase and proper-case versions became exact copies, so audit again after every fix.

47
00:03:23,428 --> 00:03:24,571
Question four:

48
00:03:24,571 --> 00:03:30,285
accuracy dropped when you added class weights, yet the model improved.

49
00:03:30,285 --> 00:03:30,611
Why?

50
00:03:30,611 --> 00:03:31,183
Answer:

51
00:03:31,183 --> 00:03:37,223
recall on the rare class rose, and accuracy mostly measures the big class.

52
00:03:37,223 --> 00:03:38,366
Question five:

53
00:03:38,366 --> 00:03:42,448
why should target encoding use only training rows?

54
00:03:42,448 --> 00:03:43,019
Answer:

55
00:03:43,019 --> 00:03:48,080
otherwise the encoding peeks at the answers, which is leakage.

56
00:03:48,880 --> 00:03:50,716
That is module three.

57
00:03:50,716 --> 00:04:04,878
Audit, then diagnose, then fix, then validate; explain why data is missing before filling it; fix outliers by cause; encode by type; and re-audit after every fix.

58
00:04:04,878 --> 00:04:06,452
Next, module four.

59
00:04:06,452 --> 00:04:08,200
Thanks for watching.

